---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="One Covering for All — Daf Yomi Game: Chullin 86"
  description="Chullin 86: a deaf-mute, an imbecile, or a minor slaughters an animal unsupervised — must someone cover the blood? Fill in Rebbi's real verdict grid, test whether a purity shortcut transfers to forbidden meat, then run a hundred animals through one covering pit and watch Rabbi Yehuda split it in two."
>
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Frank+Ruhl+Libre:wght@400;700;900&display=swap"
  />

  <main class="min-h-screen bg-[#0d0a08] !text-[#ece4d8]">
    <!-- ============ Hero ============ -->
    <div class="border-b border-[#34241a] bg-gradient-to-b from-[#1a1108]/80 to-transparent px-4 pt-10 pb-8 text-center">
      <p class="mb-3 text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] !text-[#c98a52]">
        <a href="/daf" class="hover:!text-[#e0a56d]">daf yomi arcade</a> · chullin 86 · 2026-07-26
      </p>
      <h1 class="frank text-4xl font-black tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">🕳️ One Covering for All</h1>
      <p class="frank mt-1 text-xl !text-[#c98a52]" dir="rtl" lang="he">כִּסּוּי אֶחָד לְכוּלָּן</p>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-1 text-xs italic !text-[#a89484]">"One covering suffices for all of them" — Chullin 86b:7</p>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c9bcac]">
        A deaf-mute, an imbecile, or a minor slaughters a wild animal with nobody watching. Must someone still
        cover its blood? May you slaughter its offspring the same day? The mishna's plain text makes it look like
        the Sages only argue about half of that question — dig into the Gemara and the truth is messier. Then:
        drop a hundred animals into one pit, one covering of earth, and watch Rabbi Yehuda insist on splitting it
        into two.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 pb-16 pt-8">
      <!-- ============ Context card ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#34241a] bg-[#18120c] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#c98a52]">before you drop the blade</p>
        <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#241a12] bg-[#120d09] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#e0a56d]">🩸 kisui hadam</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#a89484]">
              The mitzva to cover the blood of a slaughtered undomesticated animal (<em>chaya</em>) or bird
              (<em>of</em>) with earth (Leviticus 17:13).
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#241a12] bg-[#120d09] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#e0a56d]">🧏 cheresh, shoteh, katan</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#a89484]">
              A deaf-mute, an imbecile, or a minor — halakhically not held responsible for their own actions.
              Their slaughter is valid only if competent adults supervise and confirm it was done properly.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#241a12] bg-[#120d09] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#e0a56d]">🐐 oto v'et beno</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#a89484]">
              "It and its offspring you shall not slaughter in one day" (Leviticus 22:28). Today's mishna asks:
              does an unsupervised slaughter of the mother trigger this prohibition for the offspring?
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#241a12] bg-[#120d09] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#e0a56d]">➗ rov / mi'ut</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#a89484]">
              Majority (<em>rov</em>) vs. minority (<em>mi'ut</em>). A recurring question on this daf: can a mere
              minority, propped up by a default legal status (<em>chazakah</em>), do the work of a majority?
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Score bar ============ -->
      <div class="sticky top-0 z-10 mb-6 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-5 gap-y-1 rounded-xl border border-[#34241a] bg-[#0d0a08]/95 px-4 py-2.5 text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] backdrop-blur">
        <span class="!text-[#a89484]">progress:</span>
        <span id="score-grid" class="!text-[#c98a52]">grid 0/6</span>
        <span id="score-twist" class="!text-[#c98a52]">twists 0/2</span>
        <span id="score-transplant" class="!text-[#c98a52]">transplant: untested</span>
        <span id="score-pit" class="!text-[#c98a52]">coverings used: 0</span>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Activity 1: Verdict Grid ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#34241a] bg-[#18120c] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#c98a52]">activity 1 · the verdict grid — Chullin 86a:6–7, 86a:14</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c9bcac]">
          The mishna reads like a simple split: everyone agrees on the first line, and only Rabbi Meir and the
          Rabbis argue about the second. Tap each cell to reveal the real ruling — the Gemara's actual conclusion
          is not what the plain text suggests.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] border-separate border-spacing-2 text-center text-xs">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="!text-[#a89484] font-bold"></th>
                <th class="!text-[#e0a56d] pb-1 font-bold">Supervised<br /><span class="!text-[#a89484] font-normal">(witnessed)</span></th>
                <th class="!text-[#e0a56d] pb-1 font-bold">Unsupervised<br /><span class="!text-[#a89484] font-normal">Rabbi Meir</span></th>
                <th class="!text-[#e0a56d] pb-1 font-bold">Unsupervised<br /><span class="!text-[#a89484] font-normal">Rabbanan (final)</span></th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td class="!text-[#e0a56d] pr-2 text-left font-bold">Cover the blood?</td>
                <td><button class="grid-cell" data-cell="sup-cover" data-answer="Obligated" data-ref="Chullin 86a:6" data-detail="Others watched and confirmed the slaughter was valid — the mitzva of kisui hadam applies like any ordinary shechita.">?</button></td>
                <td><button class="grid-cell" data-cell="meir-cover" data-answer="Exempt" data-ref="Chullin 86a:6, 86a:14" data-detail="Unsupervised, Rabbi Meir holds this was never a valid slaughter at all — so there's nothing to cover.">?</button></td>
                <td><button class="grid-cell" data-cell="rab-cover" data-answer="Obligated" data-ref="Chullin 86a:14" data-detail="The Gemara's dialectic (86a:8–13) tries reading the mishna as if the Rabbis conceded this line — but concludes at 86a:14 that they actually dispute it too: they hold you must cover, since the slaughter might well have been valid.">?</button></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="!text-[#e0a56d] pr-2 text-left font-bold">Slaughter the offspring after?</td>
                <td><button class="grid-cell" data-cell="sup-off" data-answer="Forbidden" data-ref="Chullin 86a:7" data-detail="Both Rabbi Meir and the Rabbis agree: a witnessed, valid slaughter of the mother triggers oto v'et beno for the offspring.">?</button></td>
                <td><button class="grid-cell" data-cell="meir-off" data-answer="Permitted" data-ref="Chullin 86a:7" data-detail="Rabbi Meir: since he treats the unsupervised slaughter as not a real slaughter, oto v'et beno never triggered — slaughter the offspring freely.">?</button></td>
                <td><button class="grid-cell" data-cell="rab-off" data-answer="Forbidden" data-ref="Chullin 86a:7, 86a:14" data-detail="The Rabbis: it might have been a valid slaughter, so oto v'et beno might apply — forbidden out of doubt.">?</button></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <div id="grid-explain" class="mt-3 hidden rounded-xl border border-[#241a12] bg-[#120d09] p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#c9bcac]"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Twist cards ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#34241a] bg-[#18120c] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#c98a52]">the twist — who's really the stringent one?</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#a89484]">
          On the grid, Rabbi Meir looks like the lenient one and the Rabbis look strict. Two more true/false calls
          flip that picture.
        </p>

        <div id="twist-1" class="twist-card mt-4 rounded-xl border border-[#241a12] bg-[#120d09] p-3" data-correct="false">
          <p class="text-sm !text-[#ece4d8]">"If someone breaks the Rabbis' ruling and slaughters the offspring anyway, he gets lashes for oto v'et beno."</p>
          <div class="mt-3 flex gap-2">
            <button class="tf-btn" data-value="true">True</button>
            <button class="tf-btn" data-value="false">False</button>
          </div>
          <div class="tf-verdict hidden mt-3 rounded-lg border p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed"></div>
        </div>

        <div id="twist-2" class="twist-card mt-4 rounded-xl border border-[#241a12] bg-[#120d09] p-3" data-correct="false">
          <p class="text-sm !text-[#ece4d8]">"Rabbi Meir is lenient all the way down — even eating the meat from the original unsupervised slaughter carries no penalty according to him."</p>
          <div class="mt-3 flex gap-2">
            <button class="tf-btn" data-value="true">True</button>
            <button class="tf-btn" data-value="false">False</button>
          </div>
          <div class="tf-verdict hidden mt-3 rounded-lg border p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed"></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Activity 2: Rov Transplant ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#34241a] bg-[#18120c] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#c98a52]">activity 2 · the rov transplant — Chullin 86a:17–86b:2</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c9bcac]">
          <strong class="!text-[#ece4d8]">The precedent (Mishnah Teharot 3:8):</strong> a ritually impure child is
          found next to pure dough, holding a scrap that might have come from it. Most children (<em>rov</em>)
          do touch things within reach — which should mean the dough is now impure. Yet <strong class="!text-[#ece4d8]">Rabbi Meir rules the dough stays pure</strong>: he appends the minority
          (<em>mi'ut</em>) of children who don't touch things to the dough's own default status
          (<em>chazakat taharah</em>), and that combination is enough to outweigh the majority against him.
          The Rabbis disagree and rule it impure.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c9bcac]">
          <strong class="!text-[#ece4d8]">Rav Pappa's challenge:</strong> if a mi'ut-plus-chazakah is enough for
          Rabbi Meir there, why does Rabbi Ami (86a:16) need a full <em>rov</em> of bungled slaughters before
          Rabbi Meir will call meat from a deaf-mute, imbecile, or minor an outright carcass? Shouldn't a mere
          mi'ut of bungled slaughters, appended to the animal's own pre-slaughter forbidden status, already do
          the job?
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide !text-[#e0a56d]">Does the purity shortcut transfer to a forbidden-food question?</p>
        <div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <button class="transplant-btn" data-value="yes">Yes — it transfers</button>
          <button class="transplant-btn" data-value="no">No — it doesn't transfer</button>
        </div>
        <div id="transplant-verdict" class="hidden mt-3 rounded-xl border p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Activity 3: The Covering Pit ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#34241a] bg-[#18120c] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#c98a52]">activity 3 · the covering pit — Chullin 86b:7–11</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c9bcac]">
          The new mishna: slaughter a hundred wild animals in one place — one covering of earth suffices for all
          of them. A hundred birds — one covering suffices. A wild animal <em>and</em> a bird together — still
          one covering. <strong class="!text-[#ece4d8]">Rabbi Yehuda dissents on the mixed case</strong>: if you
          slaughter a wild animal, you must cover its blood before you may slaughter a bird at all.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#a89484]">
          The verse reads "an undomesticated animal <strong class="!text-[#ece4d8]">or</strong> a bird" (Leviticus
          17:13) — Rabbi Yehuda reads "or" as dividing the two categories. The Rabbis read the next verse instead
          ("the blood of all flesh is bound up with its life," 17:14) as proof that "its blood" already covers
          any quantity, so "or" must simply mean the mitzva applies whichever one you slaughter — not that they
          need separate coverings.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <button id="mode-rabbanan" class="mode-btn active">Rabbanan — one pit for everyone</button>
          <button id="mode-yehuda" class="mode-btn">Rabbi Yehuda — chaya first, then bird</button>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <button id="add-chaya" class="pit-action-btn">🦌 Slaughter a wild animal</button>
          <button id="add-of" class="pit-action-btn">🐦 Slaughter a bird</button>
          <button id="cover-blood" class="pit-action-btn cover">🪨 Cover the blood</button>
          <button id="reset-pit" class="pit-action-btn reset">↺ Reset</button>
        </div>

        <div id="pit-warning" class="hidden mt-3 rounded-lg border border-[#5a2a1e] bg-[#1c0e0a] p-2.5 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#e2867a]"></div>

        <div id="pit-area" class="mt-4 flex min-h-[64px] flex-wrap items-center gap-2 rounded-xl border border-dashed border-[#34241a] bg-[#100c08] p-3"></div>

        <p class="mt-3 text-xs !text-[#a89484]">
          animals in pit: <span id="pit-count">0</span> · coverings used: <span id="pit-coverings">0</span> ·
          <span id="pit-goal" class="!text-[#e0a56d]"></span>
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-[#241a12] bg-[#120d09] p-3">
          <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#e0a56d]">bonus — does covering interrupt the blessing?</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#a89484]">
            Rabbi Yehuda forces you to stop mid-batch and cover the wild animal's blood before slaughtering the
            bird. Real interruption, right in the middle of your two acts of shechita. True or false: Rabbi
            Yehuda requires a new blessing before slaughtering the bird.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-3 flex gap-2">
            <button class="tf-btn" data-value="true" id="bracha-true">True</button>
            <button class="tf-btn" data-value="false" id="bracha-false">False</button>
          </div>
          <div id="bracha-verdict" class="hidden mt-3 rounded-lg border p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed"></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Epilogue: Which Ruling Was Final? ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#34241a] bg-[#18120c] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#c98a52]">epilogue · which ruling was final? — Chullin 86b:3–6</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c9bcac]">
          Rebbi (Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi) himself ruled <strong class="!text-[#ece4d8]">like Rabbi Meir</strong> at one
          point — and <strong class="!text-[#ece4d8]">like the Rabbis</strong> at another. Same dispute, two
          contradictory rulings from the same man. Which one did he mean to stick with?
        </p>
        <div id="epilogue-clues" class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-2"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Learn the real daf ============ -->
      <div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-[#34241a] bg-[#18120c] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#c98a52]">learn the real daf</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c9bcac]">
          86a opens by wrapping up 85's leftover question — why Rebbi told Rabbi Chiyya to render the bird a
          tereifa rather than simply tear its windpipe, or vice versa, depending on which tradition (Rav Dimi's or
          Ravin's) you follow — and then questions whether moths could really have touched Rabbi Chiyya's flax at
          all, given that his merit was said to banish such things; the answer: his merit protected everyone
          <em>except himself</em>, like Chanina ben Dosa living on a kav of carobs while sustaining the world
          (<a class="!text-[#e0a56d] underline hover:!text-[#f0c294]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.86a.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">86a:1–5</a>).
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c9bcac]">
          Then the mishna and dialectic this game is built from: a deaf-mute, an imbecile, or a minor's
          unsupervised slaughter, the covering-the-blood and oto-v'et-beno questions it raises, and the
          surprising conclusion that Rabbi Meir — who looks lenient — actually holds it's certainly forbidden meat,
          while the Rabbis' apparent stringency rests entirely on doubt
          (<a class="!text-[#e0a56d] underline hover:!text-[#f0c294]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.86a.6" target="_blank" rel="noopener">86a:6–18</a>,
          <a class="!text-[#e0a56d] underline hover:!text-[#f0c294]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.86b.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">86b:1–6</a>).
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c9bcac]">
          Amud b closes with the new mishna on covering the blood of many animals at once, Rabbi Yehuda's dissent,
          the dueling derivations from "an undomesticated animal or a bird" and "the blood of all flesh," and
          Rabbi Chanina's concession about the blessing — cut off mid-question, to be resolved on tomorrow's daf
          (<a class="!text-[#e0a56d] underline hover:!text-[#f0c294]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.86b.7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">86b:7–13</a>).
        </p>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.86a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#c98a52]/40 bg-[#c98a52]/10 px-3 py-1.5 text-xs font-bold !text-[#e0a56d] hover:bg-[#c98a52]/20">Chullin 86 on Sefaria →</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Rashi_on_Chullin.86a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#34241a] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#a89484] hover:!text-[#ece4d8]">Rashi (×46)</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Steinsaltz_on_Chullin.86a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#34241a] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#a89484] hover:!text-[#ece4d8]">Steinsaltz (×31)</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Rabbeinu_Gershom_on_Chullin.86a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#34241a] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#a89484] hover:!text-[#ece4d8]">Rabbeinu Gershom (×23)</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chidushei_Chatam_Sofer_on_Chullin.86a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#34241a] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#a89484] hover:!text-[#ece4d8]">Chatam Sofer (×13)</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <style>
    .frank {
      font-family: 'Frank Ruhl Libre', 'Times New Roman', serif;
    }
    .grid-cell {
      width: 100%;
      min-height: 44px;
      border: 1px solid #241a12;
      background: #100c08;
      color: #ece4d8;
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 8px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .grid-cell:hover { border-color: #c98a52; color: #e0a56d; }
    .grid-cell.revealed { cursor: default; }
    .grid-cell.revealed.yes { border-color: #b8463a; background: #1c0e0a; color: #e2867a; }
    .grid-cell.revealed.no { border-color: #7c9152; background: #10160c; color: #c3d69a; }
    .tf-btn {
      border: 1px solid #2a2018;
      background: #100c08;
      color: #ece4d8;
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 6px 16px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .tf-btn:hover { border-color: #c98a52; color: #e0a56d; }
    .tf-btn:disabled { cursor: default; opacity: 0.5; }
    .tf-verdict.hidden, .transplant-verdict.hidden { display: none; }
    .tf-verdict.correct, #transplant-verdict.correct, #bracha-verdict.correct {
      border-color: #7c9152; background: #10160c; color: #c3d69a;
    }
    .transplant-btn {
      border: 1px solid #2a2018;
      background: #100c08;
      color: #ece4d8;
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 8px 14px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .transplant-btn:hover { border-color: #c98a52; color: #e0a56d; }
    .transplant-btn:disabled { cursor: default; opacity: 0.5; }
    .mode-btn {
      border: 1px solid #2a2018;
      background: #100c08;
      color: #a89484;
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 8px 12px;
      cursor: pointer;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    }
    .mode-btn:hover { border-color: #c98a52; color: #e0a56d; }
    .mode-btn.active { border-color: #c98a52; background: #241708; color: #e0a56d; }
    .pit-action-btn {
      border: 1px solid #2a2018;
      background: #100c08;
      color: #ece4d8;
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 8px 14px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .pit-action-btn:hover { border-color: #c98a52; color: #e0a56d; }
    .pit-action-btn.cover:hover { border-color: #7c9152; color: #c3d69a; }
    .pit-action-btn.reset { opacity: 0.7; }
    .pit-token {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      width: 34px;
      height: 34px;
      border-radius: 50%;
      font-size: 16px;
      border: 2px solid #b8463a;
      background: #1c0e0a;
      position: relative;
    }
    .pit-token.covered {
      border-color: #6b4a2e;
      background: #241708;
      opacity: 0.75;
    }
    .clue-btn {
      border: 1px solid #2a2018;
      background: #100c08;
      color: #e0a56d;
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 10px 14px;
      cursor: pointer;
      text-align: left;
    }
    .clue-btn:hover { border-color: #c98a52; }
    .clue-text {
      border: 1px solid #241a12;
      background: #120d09;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 10px 14px;
      font-size: 12px;
      line-height: 1.6;
      color: #c9bcac;
    }
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      .fade-in { animation: fadeIn 0.3s ease both; }
      @keyframes fadeIn { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(4px); } to { opacity: 1; transform: none; } }
    }
  </style>

  <script>
    const $ = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id);
    const $$ = (sel: string, root: ParentNode = document) => Array.from(root.querySelectorAll(sel));

    // ==================================================================
    // Activity 1 — The Verdict Grid, Chullin 86a:6-7 & the Gemara's
    // conclusion at 86a:14 that the Rabbis actually dispute BOTH clauses
    // of the mishna, not just the oto v'et beno clause.
    // ==================================================================
    let gridRevealed = 0;
    $$('.grid-cell').forEach((btn) => {
      btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        if (btn.classList.contains('revealed')) return;
        const answer = btn.getAttribute('data-answer') || '';
        const ref = btn.getAttribute('data-ref') || '';
        const detail = btn.getAttribute('data-detail') || '';
        btn.textContent = answer;
        btn.classList.add('revealed', 'fade-in');
        btn.classList.add(answer === 'Obligated' || answer === 'Forbidden' ? 'yes' : 'no');
        const explain = $('grid-explain');
        if (explain) {
          explain.classList.remove('hidden');
          explain.classList.add('fade-in');
          explain.innerHTML = `<strong class="!text-[#ece4d8]">${answer}</strong> — ${detail} <span class="!text-[#a89484]">(${ref})</span>`;
        }
        gridRevealed += 1;
        const scoreEl = $('score-grid');
        if (scoreEl) scoreEl.textContent = `grid ${gridRevealed}/6`;
      });
    });

    // ==================================================================
    // Twist cards — Chullin 86a:7 (no lashes concession) and 86a:15-16
    // (Rabbi Meir's actual stringency: lashes for neveila, no doubt at
    // all, "since the majority of their actions are bungled").
    // ==================================================================
    let twistCount = 0;
    const TWIST_EXPLAIN: Record<string, string> = {
      'twist-1': '<strong>False.</strong> The mishna itself says the Rabbis concede: if one violates their ruling and slaughters the offspring anyway, he does not incur lashes — because it is possible the mother\'s slaughter was never valid to begin with, so the oto v\'et beno prohibition may never have triggered (Chullin 86a:7).',
      'twist-2': '<strong>False.</strong> The opposite: Rabbi Yaakov reports in Rabbi Yochanan\'s name that Rabbi Meir would obligate lashes for eating this meat, as a carcass (neveila) — with no doubt at all. Rabbi Ami explains why: "since the majority of their actions are bungled," Rabbi Meir treats their unsupervised slaughter as certainly invalid, not merely doubtful (Chullin 86a:15-16).',
    };
    $$('.twist-card').forEach((card) => {
      const correct = card.getAttribute('data-correct');
      let answered = false;
      $$('.tf-btn', card).forEach((btn) => {
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          if (answered) return;
          answered = true;
          const val = btn.getAttribute('data-value');
          const verdict = card.querySelector('.tf-verdict');
          $$('.tf-btn', card).forEach((b) => ((b as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true));
          (btn as HTMLElement).style.borderColor = val === correct ? '#7c9152' : '#b8463a';
          (btn as HTMLElement).style.color = val === correct ? '#c3d69a' : '#e2867a';
          if (verdict) {
            verdict.classList.remove('hidden');
            verdict.classList.add('correct', 'fade-in');
            verdict.innerHTML = TWIST_EXPLAIN[card.id] || '';
          }
          twistCount += 1;
          const scoreEl = $('score-twist');
          if (scoreEl) scoreEl.textContent = `twists ${twistCount}/2`;
        });
      });
    });

    // ==================================================================
    // Activity 2 — The Rov Transplant, Chullin 86a:17-86b:2. Rav Pappa
    // asks whether Rabbi Meir's mi'ut-plus-chazakah move (from Mishnah
    // Teharot 3:8) transfers from a doubt of impurity to a doubt of
    // forbidden food. The Gemara answers: no.
    // ==================================================================
    $$('.transplant-btn').forEach((btn) => {
      btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        if ((btn as HTMLButtonElement).disabled) return;
        $$('.transplant-btn').forEach((b) => ((b as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true));
        const val = btn.getAttribute('data-value');
        const verdict = $('transplant-verdict');
        $$('.transplant-btn').forEach((b) => {
          (b as HTMLElement).style.borderColor = b.getAttribute('data-value') === 'no' ? '#7c9152' : '';
          (b as HTMLElement).style.color = b.getAttribute('data-value') === 'no' ? '#c3d69a' : '';
        });
        if (verdict) {
          verdict.classList.remove('hidden');
          verdict.classList.add('correct', 'fade-in');
          verdict.innerHTML = val === 'no'
            ? '<strong>Correct — it doesn\'t transfer.</strong> The Gemara rejects the transplant: "If they said [rely on mi\'ut-plus-chazakah] for a doubt of impurity, in order to rule leniently — will they say the same for a doubt of prohibition, in order to permit it?" (Chullin 86b:2). A shortcut validated for questions of ritual purity doesn\'t automatically carry over to questions of forbidden food. Rabbi Meir\'s stringency here genuinely needs Rabbi Ami\'s full rov of bungled actions.'
            : '<strong>Not quite — the Gemara says it doesn\'t transfer.</strong> "If they said [rely on mi\'ut-plus-chazakah] for a doubt of impurity, in order to rule leniently — will they say the same for a doubt of prohibition, in order to permit it?" (Chullin 86b:2). A shortcut validated for ritual purity doesn\'t automatically carry over to forbidden food. Rabbi Meir\'s stringency here genuinely needs Rabbi Ami\'s full rov of bungled actions.';
        }
        const scoreEl = $('score-transplant');
        if (scoreEl) scoreEl.textContent = `transplant: tested`;
      });
    });

    // ==================================================================
    // Activity 3 — The Covering Pit, Chullin 86b:7-11. Rabbanan: one
    // covering handles any mix of wild animals and birds. Rabbi Yehuda:
    // must cover the wild animal's blood before slaughtering a bird
    // (derived from "an undomesticated animal OR a bird," read as
    // dividing the two categories, Chullin 86b:9).
    // ==================================================================
    type Animal = { type: 'chaya' | 'of'; covered: boolean };
    let mode: 'rabbanan' | 'yehuda' = 'rabbanan';
    let animals: Animal[] = [];
    let coveringsUsed = 0;

    function renderPit() {
      const area = $('pit-area');
      if (area) {
        area.innerHTML = '';
        animals.forEach((a) => {
          const el = document.createElement('div');
          el.className = 'pit-token' + (a.covered ? ' covered' : '');
          el.textContent = a.type === 'chaya' ? '🦌' : '🐦';
          el.title = a.covered ? 'blood covered' : 'blood uncovered';
          area.appendChild(el);
        });
      }
      const countEl = $('pit-count');
      if (countEl) countEl.textContent = String(animals.length);
      const coveringsEl = $('pit-coverings');
      if (coveringsEl) coveringsEl.textContent = String(coveringsUsed);
      const scoreEl = $('score-pit');
      if (scoreEl) scoreEl.textContent = `coverings used: ${coveringsUsed}`;
      const goalEl = $('pit-goal');
      if (goalEl) {
        goalEl.textContent = mode === 'rabbanan'
          ? 'goal: any mix, just 1 covering total'
          : 'goal: all wild animals, cover, then all birds — 2 coverings';
      }
    }

    function showWarning(msg: string) {
      const warn = $('pit-warning');
      if (warn) {
        warn.textContent = msg;
        warn.classList.remove('hidden');
        warn.classList.add('fade-in');
      }
    }
    function hideWarning() {
      const warn = $('pit-warning');
      if (warn) warn.classList.add('hidden');
    }

    $('add-chaya')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      hideWarning();
      animals.push({ type: 'chaya', covered: false });
      renderPit();
    });

    $('add-of')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      if (mode === 'yehuda' && animals.some((a) => a.type === 'chaya' && !a.covered)) {
        showWarning('Rabbi Yehuda: "Slaughter a wild animal — cover it, and only afterward slaughter the bird." Cover the blood first (Chullin 86b:9).');
        return;
      }
      hideWarning();
      animals.push({ type: 'of', covered: false });
      renderPit();
    });

    $('cover-blood')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      hideWarning();
      const uncovered = animals.filter((a) => !a.covered);
      if (uncovered.length === 0) return;
      uncovered.forEach((a) => (a.covered = true));
      coveringsUsed += 1;
      renderPit();
    });

    $('reset-pit')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      hideWarning();
      animals = [];
      coveringsUsed = 0;
      renderPit();
    });

    function setMode(next: 'rabbanan' | 'yehuda') {
      mode = next;
      $('mode-rabbanan')?.classList.toggle('active', mode === 'rabbanan');
      $('mode-yehuda')?.classList.toggle('active', mode === 'yehuda');
      hideWarning();
      renderPit();
    }
    $('mode-rabbanan')?.addEventListener('click', () => setMode('rabbanan'));
    $('mode-yehuda')?.addEventListener('click', () => setMode('yehuda'));

    renderPit();

    // Bonus — Rabbi Chanina's blessing concession, Chullin 86b:12-13.
    let brachaAnswered = false;
    $$('#bracha-true, #bracha-false').forEach((btn) => {
      btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        if (brachaAnswered) return;
        brachaAnswered = true;
        const val = btn.getAttribute('data-value');
        const verdict = $('bracha-verdict');
        ($('bracha-true') as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true;
        ($('bracha-false') as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true;
        (btn as HTMLElement).style.borderColor = val === 'false' ? '#7c9152' : '#b8463a';
        (btn as HTMLElement).style.color = val === 'false' ? '#c3d69a' : '#e2867a';
        if (verdict) {
          verdict.classList.remove('hidden');
          verdict.classList.add('correct', 'fade-in');
          verdict.innerHTML = '<strong>False.</strong> Rabbi Chanina says Rabbi Yehuda concedes only one blessing is needed for both acts of shechita (Chullin 86b:12). Ravina then pushes back with a parallel: Rav Beruna and Rav Chananel, students of Rav, once asked for a cup "to bless" over and then changed their minds to just drink from it — and were told that declaring "give me a cup to bless" already ends the meal, requiring a new blessing to drink more (Chullin 86b:13). Ravina\'s question — why covering the blood mid-shechita doesn\'t count as the same kind of interruption — is left open. The daf ends here; the answer waits for tomorrow.';
        }
      });
    });

    // ==================================================================
    // Epilogue — Chullin 86b:3-6. Rebbi ruled like both Rabbi Meir and
    // the Rabbis at different times; the Gemara traces which was final
    // through the Caesarea study-hall story and Rabbi Elazar's letter.
    // ==================================================================
    const CLUES = [
      {
        label: 'Reveal clue 1 — the marketplace at Caesarea',
        text: 'Rabbi Abba (son of Rabbi Chiyya bar Abba) and Rabbi Zeira were standing in the marketplace of Caesarea, right at the entrance to the study hall. Rabbi Ami came out and found them there and scolded them: "Didn\'t I tell you not to stand outside while the study hall is in session? Someone inside might need help with a difficult point, and you won\'t be there."',
      },
      {
        label: 'Reveal clue 2 — one goes in, one stays outside',
        text: 'Rabbi Zeira went in; Rabbi Abba did not. Inside, the students were sitting and debating this very question — which of Rebbi\'s two rulings was the later, final one? Rabbi Zeira said to them: you should have told me this was your question while I was still outside — I would have asked the elder, Rabbi Abba, since his father, Rabbi Chiyya bar Abba, reviewed his learning in front of Rabbi Yochanan every thirty days, and might well have known the answer.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Reveal clue 3 — the letter to the Diaspora',
        text: 'The resolution comes from elsewhere: Rabbi Elazar sent word to the Jews in Babylonia: "Rebbi ruled like Rabbi Meir." The Gemara objects — but Rebbi also ruled like the Rabbis! The conclusion: since Rabbi Elazar reported only the Meir version as the operative one, that must be the later, final ruling (Chullin 86b:6).',
      },
    ];
    const cluesContainer = $('epilogue-clues');
    if (cluesContainer) {
      CLUES.forEach((clue, i) => {
        const btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.className = 'clue-btn';
        btn.textContent = clue.label;
        const textEl = document.createElement('div');
        textEl.className = 'clue-text hidden';
        textEl.textContent = clue.text;
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          textEl.classList.remove('hidden');
          textEl.classList.add('fade-in');
          btn.disabled = true;
          btn.style.opacity = '0.6';
        });
        cluesContainer.appendChild(btn);
        cluesContainer.appendChild(textEl);
      });
    }
  </script>
</Layout>
